<Group labelreference="L104" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"><Font bold="true">Abstract</Font></Text-field><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Font encoding="UTF-8"> One of the first applications of Schr\303\266dinger Equation covered during any introductory course on Quantum Mechanics, has to do with scattering problems where a particle collides with a potential barrier. This barrier can be infinite, finite, or a portion of a potential step. In one dimension, the problem allows to demonstrate phenomena unique to quantum mechanics, such as quantum tunnelling. In this project, we analyze this problem in a two-dimensional setting: an initially Gaussian profile moves towards a two-dimensional potential barrier. We will solve the two-dimensional time-dependent Schr\303\266dinger equation using the Crank-Nicholson method. The choice of a Gaussian as an initial condition is based on the fact that this profile resembles the most classical particle fairly localized in both position and momentum. This project will also allow us to study the tunnelling phenomena in two dimensions.</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L5" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
