<Group labelreference="L529" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The problem at hand consists of a partial differential equation, of first order in time and second order in the space derivatives. Therefore, we requiere an initial condition, as well as boundary conditions.

The motivation for our choice of a particular profile for the initial condition, comes from Classical Mechanics: We want to model a particle moving towards the potential step, so our starting point is a wave moving towards the positive x direction <Equation executable="false" style="Text" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>However, this is far from a classical particle, as it consists of  a fucntion oscillating everywhere in the plane. In Quantum Mechanics it is not possible to localize a physical system neither in position or in momentum because of he uncertainty relation between these two quantities.  What it is usually done instead when trying to replicate a classical particle through a quantum state, is to use a gaussian wave function. We will therefore localize our right moving wave thorugh a gaussian amplitud of the form <Equation executable="false" style="Text" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>
</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L236" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">psi(x,y,t=0)=A[0]*exp(-((x - x0)^2 + (y - y0)^2)/sigma)*exp(k*x*I);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">Ly1JJHBzaUc2IjYlSSJ4R0YlSSJ5R0YlL0kidEdGJSIiISooJkkiQUdGJTYjRisiIiItSSRleHBHNiQlKnByb3RlY3RlZEdJKF9zeXNsaWJHRiU2IywkKiYsJiokKSwmRidGMEkjeDBHRiUhIiIiIiNGMEYwKiQpLCZGKEYwSSN5MEdGJUY+Rj9GMEYwRjBJJnNpZ21hR0YlRj5GPkYwLUYyNiMqKF4jRjBGMEkia0dGJUYwRidGMEYw</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L550" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Font encoding="UTF-8">The presence of A0 in our initial state comes from the fact that, |\317\210|</Font><Equation executable="false" style="Text" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>physically interpreted as the probability density of the system of being located in a certain region of space <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlElZHhkeUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnRjIvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlElZHhkeUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnRjIvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn</Equation>. So the integral of this probability density over the entire plane should be 1. A0 is basically a normalization constant ensuring this initial state can be interpreted as a probabilty density. For this particular case, <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>The corresponding initial probability density <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>is a real function that plotted on the plane looks as follows</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L10" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">psi0:=(x,y)-&gt;2/evalf(Pi)*exp(-2*((x+5)^2+(y)^2));</Text-field>
