<Group labelreference="L581" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Font encoding="UTF-8">Finally, the boundary conditions are chosen so that the wave function vanishes at the boundaries. The physical motivation for this choice comes from the fact that in Quantum Mechanics one only deals with normalizable-square-integrable functions so that its norm squared can be interpreted as a probability density, so we discard the posibibilities where \317\210 could blow up at infinity for example. The boundary conditions will then be as follows
</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L582" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"><Font encoding="UTF-8">\317\210(x=10,y,t)=\317\210(x=-10,y,t)=\317\210(x,y=10,t)=\317\210(x,y=-10,t)=0</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L583" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">This concludes our set up of the problem.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
