<Group labelreference="L729" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The previous simulation is composed of plots of the probability density, which is a real quantity, over the XY plane. We can observe that the particle moves towards the potential barrier as expected, but when it gets to the it, part of the probability density gets reflected and part of it gets transmitted. 

We can also observe that whenever the solution reaches one of the boundaries, it bounces back, producing some undesired interference with itself. This effect is an artifact of the size of the spatial grid. The bigger the grid, the longer it will take for the solution to reach the boundaries and these unwanted reflections will occur at a later time.

Notice as well that our initial profile had <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>. Without getting into the details, this k is like the initial momentum of the wave, and is therefore related to the initial energy of the system via <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation><Font encoding="UTF-8"> For our choice of \342\204\217=1, and </Font><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>this means that <Equation executable="true" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>corresponds to an energy less than the height of the potential step <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>We will now generate the same simulation but for a value of k that corresponds to an initial energy bigger than the potential.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
