<Group labelreference="L742" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">On this second simulation we can observe that a bigger portion of the solution gets through the potential barrier. This is in agreement with our classical intuition, as a particle with enough energy will just continue moving despite the potential barrier. It should be noted however that part of the solution still bounces from the potential, against our classical intuition, so this effect is pureley quantum-mechanical.

Another important aspect to point out is that in this second simulation the reflections on the boundaries take more time to make themselves present in the plots. This is because, since our inital state has more initial energy, it's moving faster towards the barrier so the collision occurs earlier than in the first simulation. This means that the wave function doesn't have as much time to spread towards the boundaries before the collission compared to the previous case. The reflections eventually do show up
</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L734" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
