<Section collapsed="false" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">Conclusions</Text-field></Title>
<Group labelreference="L745" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L746" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Font encoding="UTF-8">In summary, the simulations obtained by numerically solving the Schr\303\266dinger equation for a farily localized quantum system moving towards a potential barrier, fulfill the expected results from a classical physical intuition, while also revealing the presence of effects due to Quantum Mechanics, such as the transmission of the solution beyond the barrier for a state with an initial energy smaller that the barrier's height, and the reflection of part of the solution for the case where the initial energy is greater than the potential.
</Font>
In the numerical aspects, the presence of reflections of the solution at the boundaries of the two-dimensional grid show an aspect that's often present on this kind of simulations. The natural approach to avoid these unwanted reflections is to increase the grid size, so that the boundaries are further away from the initial state, and it takes more time to reach them. In this way, the physical phenomena that's being modeled, like the collision with the barrier in this case, can have enough time to occurr before the reflections at the boundaries appear and start interfering with the solution deep inside the grid. In the second simulation this is mostly the case for  great part of the animation. In the first simulation however, the reflections do show up relatively early, and it could benefit from a bigger grid. However, the computational cost of increasing the grid size should also be taken into account.

<Font encoding="UTF-8">One final remark. This code could also be used to solve Schr\303\266dinger equation for a different potential and a different initial profile, by changing the corresponding definitions of those functions within the code.</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
</Section>
</Section>
<Section collapsed="false" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 1" layout="Heading 1">Appendix</Text-field></Title>
<Group labelreference="L749" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Font encoding="UTF-8">On This appendix we will check norm preservation of the wave fucntion throughout the evolution dictatetd by our numerical solution. The Initial data provided to the problem is initially normalized, so that it can have the physical interpretation opf a density probability. In Schr\303\266dinger equation, all the operators that act on the wave function are unitary operators, so mathematically speaking the norm is preserved through the evolution according to that equation. For numerical methods however, since instead of differential operators one is using finiute difference operators as an approximations, the preservation of the norm is not guaranteed. To test this preservation, let's use an example for a spatial grid of 10x10 points, and a time evolution in 10 time steps.</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
